Below is a **complete, self‑contained console application** that shows the core patterns of Entity Framework Core (EF Core).  
It uses SQLite (in‑memory for quick demo) and demonstrates:

1. **Entity & POCO** – `Blog` & `Post`
2. **DbContext** – `BloggingContext`
3. **Fluent API** – relationships & constraints  
4. **Database creation** (`EnsureCreated`)
5. **CRUD** – create, read, update, delete
6. **Eager loading** (`Include`)
7. **Async LINQ** – modern EF usage

> **Prerequisites**  
> * .NET 6/7 SDK (you can drop this into a new console project).  
> * NuGet packages:  
>   ```
>   dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore
>   dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite
>   ```

```csharp
// Program.cs
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

namespace EfCoreDemo
{
    // 1. Domain entities
    public class Blog
    {
        public int BlogId { get; set; }               // PK
        public string Url { get; set; } = string.Empty;

        // Navigation property – one-to-many
        public List<Post> Posts { get; set; } = new();
    }

    public class Post
    {
        public int PostId { get; set; }               // PK
        public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;
        public string Content { get; set; } = string.Empty;

        // FK
        public int BlogId { get; set; }
        public Blog? Blog { get; set; }               // Navigation
    }

    // 2. DbContext
    public class BloggingContext : DbContext
    {
        public DbSet<Blog> Blogs => Set<Blog>();
        public DbSet<Post> Posts => Set<Post>();

        // Use an in‑memory SQLite database
        protected override void OnConfiguring(DbContextOptionsBuilder options)
            => options.UseSqlite("Data Source=blogging.db");

        protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
        {
            // Optional: configure relationships and constraints
            modelBuilder.Entity<Blog>()
                .HasMany(b => b.Posts)
                .WithOne(p => p.Blog)
                .HasForeignKey(p => p.BlogId)
                .OnDelete(DeleteBehavior.Cascade);

            // Seed data (optional)
            modelBuilder.Entity<Blog>().HasData(
                new Blog { BlogId = 1, Url = "https://dotnet.microsoft.com" }
            );
            modelBuilder.Entity<Post>().HasData(
                new Post { PostId = 1, Title = "EF Core 101", Content = "Getting started with EF Core.", BlogId = 1 }
            );
        }
    }

    class Program
    {
        static async Task Main()
        {
            // 3. Ensure database exists
            await using var db = new BloggingContext();
            await db.Database.EnsureDeletedAsync();   // For demo – start fresh
            await db.Database.EnsureCreatedAsync();  // Creates tables

            // 4. Create – add a new Blog with Posts
            var blog = new Blog
            {
                Url = "https://learn.microsoft.com",
                Posts = new List<Post>
                {
                    new Post { Title = "C# 12", Content = "New language features." },
                    new Post { Title = "EF Core 7", Content = "Latest EF Core release." }
                }
            };

            await db.Blogs.AddAsync(blog);
            await db.SaveChangesAsync();
            Console.WriteLine($"Added Blog with Id {blog.BlogId}");

            // 5. Read – query with eager loading
            var blogs = await db.Blogs
                .Include(b => b.Posts)           // eager load Posts
                .OrderBy(b => b.BlogId)
                .ToListAsync();

            Console.WriteLine("\nAll blogs and their posts:");
            foreach (var b in blogs)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Blog #{b.BlogId}: {b.Url}");
                foreach (var p in b.Posts)
                {
                    Console.WriteLine($"  Post #{p.PostId}: {p.Title}");
                }
            }

            // 6. Update – modify a Post
            var postToUpdate = blogs[0].Posts.First();
            postToUpdate.Content = "Updated content for EF Core 7.";
            await db.SaveChangesAsync();
            Console.WriteLine($"\nUpdated Post #{postToUpdate.PostId}");

            // 7. Delete – remove a Blog (cascade deletes its Posts)
            var blogToDelete = blogs.Last();
            db.Blogs.Remove(blogToDelete);
            await db.SaveChangesAsync();
            Console.WriteLine($"\nDeleted Blog #{blogToDelete.BlogId}");

            // 8. Final state
            var remainingBlogs = await db.Blogs
                .Include(b => b.Posts)
                .ToListAsync();

            Console.WriteLine("\nRemaining blogs:");
            foreach (var b in remainingBlogs)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Blog #{b.BlogId}: {b.Url}");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### What you’ll see when you run it

```
Added Blog with Id 2

All blogs and their posts:
Blog #1: https://dotnet.microsoft.com
  Post #1: EF Core 101
Blog #2: https://learn.microsoft.com
  Post #2: C# 12
  Post #3: EF Core 7

Updated Post #2

Deleted Blog #1

Remaining blogs:
Blog #2: https://learn.microsoft.com
```

### Key take‑aways

| Feature | Code reference | Why it matters |
|---------|----------------|----------------|
| **Entity classes** | `Blog`, `Post` | POCOs represent tables. |
| **DbContext** | `BloggingContext` | Holds `DbSet<T>` and configures EF. |
| **Fluent API** | `OnModelCreating` | Fine‑grained control over schema, relationships, seed data. |
| **Database lifecycle** | `EnsureDeletedAsync`, `EnsureCreatedAsync` | For demo; in production use migrations. |
| **CRUD** | `AddAsync`, `SaveChangesAsync`, `Remove` | Core operations. |
| **Eager loading** | `Include` | Loads related data in one query. |
| **Async/await** | All EF methods | Non‑blocking I/O. |

Drop the code into a console project, run `dotnet run`, and you’ll have a fully working EF Core demo. Feel free to expand it – add a `User` entity, test `ManyToMany`, or switch to a full‑featured database!
